FoamFile
{
	version 2.0;
	format	ascii;
	class	dictionary;
	object	turbulentProperties;
}

simulationType	laminar;
// We choose Laminar Flow when the Reynolds number is low enough, or has ultra fine meshes. In this simulation, the solver computes the Navier Strokes Equations using zero turbulent modelling. We have two other options, RAS(Reynolds Averages Simulation RANS) -- It averages out the turbulent fluctuations ovcer time and models there effects. We use it when the Reynolds number is high, and when we only carre about the average forces, pressures or velocities. This saves time and compute. The last one is LES (Large Eddy Simulation) -- it is a brute force method to directyl sovle the large turbulent eddies in space/time. We use it when the flow is unsteady and super complex, like acoustics etc. It requires a very fine 3D Mesh and longer run times. 
